  "answer": "I can't give you October-November figures for reservations, and so I can't give a meaningful idle-cost share either.\n\n**What Capacity Manager actually returned.** I asked both presets for the maximum look-back (2,160 hours), but both results report the same window: **2026-11-16T00:00:00Z to 2026-12-02T07:00:00Z**. That is about 16 days, not the two months you need. The window starts on 2026-11-16, so October and the first half of November are not covered at all, and it runs past 2026-11-30 into 2026-12-01 and 2026-12-02. The tool clamps the window to the range the API holds data for, and this `period` is the only place that shows up.\n\nWithin that window only (2026-11-16 to 2026-12-02):\n\n| account | idle reservation cost | utilization |\n|---|---|---|\n| 604215873390 | $3,912.48 | 38.2% |\n| 731508264117 | $2,207.15 | 65.8% |\n| 289046137552 | $968.30 | 73.7% |\n| 815730492268 | $251.77 | 88.6% |\n| **total (4 accounts)** | **$7,339.70** | **63.3% overall** |\n\n(The `unused_cost` preset's own roll-up shows 0.0% utilization and $0.00 total cost because it never requests those metrics; the 63.3% comes from the `utilization` preset.)\n\n**EC2 spend (cost monitor summary):** October 2026 $148,602.35, November 2026 $152,917.80, together $301,520.15.\n\n**The share.** Dividing $7,339.70 by $301,520.15 would compare about 16 days of idle cost, partly in December, against two full months of EC2 spend. The periods don't match, so the result would understate waste and should not go in the review. To get a two-month figure, Capacity Manager would need data from 2026-10-01 onward. The review can quote the 2026-11-16 to 2026-12-02 figures above, labelled with that window, and leave out the share.\n"
